WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.489
I will sign the North American

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.949
Free Trade Act into law

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.749
that will tear down trade barriers

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:09.089
between our three nations.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.709
Once upon a time, there was a

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.629
bipartisan consensus that free trade

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.879
was good for America and the world.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.439
It will create the world's largest

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.959
free trade zone and create

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.299
200,000 jobs in this country

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.459
by 1995 alone.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.259
NAFTA eliminated most tariffs on

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.639
products in North America and

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.739
regional trade climbed from $290

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.439
billion to 1.1 trillion

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.329
over the next 20 years.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:35.159
Canada and Mexico became the two

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.809
largest destinations for U.S.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.249
exports.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.259
Although George W Bush imposed job

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.449
killing steel tariffs in 2002,

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:43.859
three years later, he signed the

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.149
Central American Free Trade

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:46.949
Agreement, eliminating most trade

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.539
barriers between the United States

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.129
and the region.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:51.839
But after the financial crisis,

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.009
Occupy Wall Street and the

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.509
resurgence of the progressive wing

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.519
of the Democratic Party, America's

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.279
free trade consensus fractured.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.779
Obama couldn't get members of his

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.549
own party to ratify the next free

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.318
trade agreement, the Trans-Pacific

00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:05.369
Partnership.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.319
The TPP, in my view, is

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.509
a continuation of disastrous

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:11.399
trade policies and not

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.389
is enough.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:14.669
We need new approaches

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.799
to trade, which creates jobs

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.349
in America and not in China.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:21.209
Democrats blamed globalization

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.279
for destroying manufacturing jobs.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.929
There's a lot of data about what's

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.399
happened in the trade agreements of

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.779
the past, and the American worker

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.369
has not done well.

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:30.989
And they blamed unemployment caused

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.699
by globalization for white working

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.589
class support for Donald Trump,

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.479
who in turn led the GOP

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.429
to stop even paying lip service

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.199
to the virtues of free trade.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.029
We're going to renegotiate

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:43.949
NAFTA, probably the

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:45.779
worst trade deal

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.329
ever agreed to sign

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:50.309
in the history of the world.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.310
The globalizers overpromised

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:54.899
and under-delivered.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.309
There's no doubt that free traders

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.349
have promised a lot when it comes

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.179
to trade agreements and the

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:01.469
benefits of trade.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:03.299
Scotland's income specializes in

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.309
trade policy at the Cato Institute.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:06.759
But in terms of under-delivering,

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:07.760
that's crazy talk.

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:12.889
Our goal as a nation

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:15.289
must be to rely less on imports

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.479
and more on products made

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.969
here in the USA.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:21.619
We're bringing manufacturing back to

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:22.909
the United States.

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:24.739
Probably the biggest myth is that

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:26.749
globalization has resulted

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.759
in the destruction of the United

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.919
States manufacturing base and

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:32.149
the destruction of American

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:33.629
manufacturing jobs.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.719
The reality is far

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:36.739
from that.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:38.029
The United States remains the second

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:39.889
largest manufacturing nation in the

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:42.289
world. Per worker output

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:43.519
in the United States is actually

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.529
higher here than it is in

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:46.729
almost every other country on the

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:47.689
planet.

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:49.189
It's true that the total number of

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:50.659
Americans working in the sector has

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:51.559
declined.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:52.909
But that's a global phenomenon

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:54.419
driven by automation.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:56.239
U.S. manufacturing output has more

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.949
than doubled since the passage of

00:02:57.950 --> 00:02:59.209
NAFTA.

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.489
We just make a lot of stuff

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:03.229
with fewer workers, and that

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:05.119
productivity is the main reason

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.189
why we've seen jobs decline

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:08.209
in manufacturing.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:10.309
It's not simply about China.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.449
We can't continue to allow

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:16.369
China to rape our

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:19.009
country. It's the greatest theft

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:20.329
in the history of the world.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:22.009
The China Shock is a series of

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.139
papers written a few years ago that

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:26.239
looked at Chinese import competition

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.639
in the United States between 1999

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.249
and 2011 and is

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:33.169
routinely used

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:34.999
to denounce not only

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.129
China trade, but also globalization

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:37.939
writ large.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:39.769
These economists found that it was

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:41.659
the cause of several

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:43.789
million American jobs

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:45.709
lost. Chinese import competition

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:47.959
surely displaced some

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:49.219
American workers.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.229
It also helped American consumers

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.329
saving American families hundreds

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:55.099
of dollars per year for the rest of

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.389
our lives.

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:58.129
That savings goes towards other

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:00.049
goods and services, creating new,

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:01.729
better paying jobs for American

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:02.989
workers.

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:04.399
A lot of the jobs that were

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.499
offshored are jobs that are simply

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.539
unfit for a high wage,

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.849
high productivity US economy.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.109
A lot of the jobs that were

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:14.389
displaced by Chinese imports

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:16.219
were on their way out

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.439
anyway, either because of changes

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:19.909
in technology and productivity here

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:21.889
in the United States or because

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:23.809
this was part of a century

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:25.789
long trend of these jobs

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:28.099
moving offshore either to China

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.649
or to other lower wage destination

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:32.179
countries like, say, Vietnam or

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:33.180
Mexico.

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:39.379
This pandemic has underscored

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:41.389
the vital importance of

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:43.249
reshoring, our supply chains and

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:44.629
bringing them back into the United

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:46.039
States where they should have never

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:47.629
left. This pandemic has really

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:49.519
illustrated the vulnerability of

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:51.739
the United States to China's control

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:52.789
of our supply chain.

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:54.469
Globalization actually, I think, has

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:56.509
been reproving during

00:04:56.510 --> 00:04:57.409
the pandemic.

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:59.269
The fact is that when you look at

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.249
the industries and the products that

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:03.109
are most affected

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:05.449
by supply chain crises

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:07.609
and shortages, whether it's baby

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:09.709
formula or COVID 19 rapid

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:11.869
tests or intermodal chases

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:13.759
or pickup trucks, you can go down

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:15.529
the list. And when you scratch those

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:16.729
shortages, you actually find

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:18.229
protectionism underneath.

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:20.179
98% of all baby formula

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:21.319
consumed in the United States is

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:22.309
made in the United States.

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:23.989
And yet shelves are bare for not

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:25.909
just days, but months at

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:27.979
a time. Baby formula is the classic

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:30.559
example. It's a terrible tragedy,

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.569
but it's a really useful lesson

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:34.669
for the perils of on

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:35.809
shoring and protectionism.

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:38.029
And imports are basically blocked.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:39.889
Not only that, you combine

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:41.839
that with high regulatory barriers

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:43.189
and strict regulation of baby

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:45.229
formula. And what happens

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:46.339
is you have a market that is

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:47.839
extremely sclerotic.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:49.729
It's unable to adjust in times

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:50.809
of crisis.

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.509
And so when a baby formula factory

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:55.369
in Michigan went offline, the

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:57.169
market couldn't suddenly adjust and

00:05:57.170 --> 00:05:58.909
adapt. Imports couldn't fill the

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:00.439
gap. We also didn't have the

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:02.239
preexisting supply and distribution

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:03.949
networks that were needed to quickly

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:06.109
fill those gaps and make our shelves

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:07.249
full again.

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:08.779
While supply chains and

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:11.209
globalization and global pandemics

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:13.099
surely carry some risks, the

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.779
risks are far greater.

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:16.459
When we put all our eggs in that

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.779
protectionist basket.

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:21.169
But free and open trade isn't just

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:22.459
good for Americans.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:24.319
It has immeasurable benefits for the

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:25.309
global poor.

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:27.169
It's a major reason why

00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:29.059
billions of humans around

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:30.859
the world have risen out of poverty.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:33.169
Our purchasing of that cheap T-shirt

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:34.969
gives those humans another

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:37.069
opportunity the opportunity to work

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:39.289
in a factory to escape abject

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:41.929
poverty and subsistence living.

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:43.789
And while we might look down

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:46.069
on those jobs, it's an immense step

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:48.169
up from starvation

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:50.119
and sex work and other types

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:51.619
of things that individuals are being

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:52.999
forced into.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:54.259
If you look at the numbers,

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:55.549
according to the International Labor

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:56.989
Organization, trade and

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:59.419
globalization have helped remove

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:01.279
billions of people from

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:02.839
that abject poverty.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:04.699
China is the most obvious example,

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:05.659
but it's not just there.

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:07.519
Sub-Saharan Africa, Bangladesh,

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:09.639
India and the rest millions.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:11.919
And millions of people are now

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:13.779
able to live more comfortable

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.029
lives, more stable and secure lives

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:17.050
because of free trade.

